  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 330 PM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Widespread showers and thunderstorms are \nexpected this afternoon and evening. The main threat is damaging\nwind gusts with large hail and a couple of tornadoes also \npossible.\n\nGOES water vapor channels depict a potent upper trough over \nNY/PA this afternoon, with a trailing shortwave axis to the SW \nthrough WV. At the surface, a cold front is pushing SE over the \nupper Ohio Valley, and there is still a subtle boundary in \nvicinity of the Ches. Bay, mainly a wind shift from SSW to SSE. \nGOES satellite channels depict vigorous convection developing \nover the mountains. The environment is becoming rather favorable\nfor strong to severe tstms this afternoon. Effective bulk \nshear, per latest SPC mesoanalysis is 35-40kt across the \nnorthern tier of the area, and SBCAPE is generally \n2000-3000J/kg. Limiting factors currently are rather poor mid- \nlevel lapse rates and minimal DCAPE. However, these are progged \nto improve over the next few hours as tstms push SE into the \nlocal area. Additionally, the mid-level flow will only \nstrengthen over the next several hours as the upper trough \napproaches from the NNW. A Severe Thunderstorm Watch has been \nissued through 10 PM for the northern half of the area, and this\ncould be expanded to include at least the counties SW of the \nRIC metro.\n\nTiming is a little later, likely due to morning cloud cover. \nHowever, the overall trend of initially more cellular convection\ntransitioning to more a a linear mode is still expected. The \nmost likely timing is from 5-7 PM N/NW, 7-9 PM from south \ncentral VA to Richmond to the MD eastern Shore, and from 8-11 PM\nin SE VA/NE NC. The main threat is damaging wind gusts, which \nwould be fairly robust whether storms remain more discrete or \nconsolidate into a line/line segments. There is also a large \nhail threat, mainly across the NW 2/3 of the area where storms \nare more likely to be discrete at the onset. Lastly, a couple of\nbrief tornadoes cannot be ruled out, especially in vicinity of \nthe aforementioned boundary where the low- level wind will be \nmore backed. SPC has maintained a Slight Risk (level 2/5) for \nthe entire area, with 30% wind probs being the most significant.\nStorm motion will be quick, which will act to limit flooding, \nbut localized flash flooding is possible, mainly in urban/flood \nprone areas, especially closer to the coast where tstms may try \nto congeal later this evening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Isolated showers and storms are possible on \nWednesday, with mainly dry weather to end the week. Temperatures\ndrop to slightly below average late this week.\n\nThere is some uncertainty in regards to rain chances in the \nwake of the cold front on Wednesday. With the closer proximity \nof the upper low, there could be some showers on Wednesday, but \nguidance continued to trend mainly dry. Thursday through Friday \nlooks mostly dry, with near to slightly below average \ntemperatures, as LLVL flow will continue to be out of the NW. \nOverall, quite pleasant for late July. Tstm chances may return \nby late this weekend as deeper moisture gradually increases, but\nit may be more into early next week.",
